{"name":"Cashmere Sweater","hex":"#fef2d2","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cashmere","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Fine, downy wool from beneath the outer hair of the Cashmere goat.","A soft fabric made of this wool."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cashmere"},{"term":"sweater","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A knitted jacket or jersey, usually of thick wool, worn by athletes before or after exercise.","A similar garment worn for warmth."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dress in a sweater."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweater"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cashmere wool","blocks":[{"text":"Cashmere wool, usually simply known as cashmere, is a fiber obtained from cashmere goats, pashmina goats, and some other breeds of goat. It has been used to make yarn, textiles and clothing for hundreds of years. Cashmere is closely associated with the Kashmir shawl, the word \"cashmere\" deriving from an anglicization of Kashmir, when the Kashmir shawl reached Europe in the 19th century. Both the soft undercoat and the guard hairs may be used; the softer hair is reserved for textiles, while the coarse guard hair is used for brushes and other non-apparel purposes. Cashmere is a hygroscopic fiber, absorbing and releasing water from the air based on the surrounding environment. This helps regulate the body in both warm and cool temperatures."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Kaschmirschal_1.jpg/330px-Kaschmirschal_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cashmere_wool","wikidata":"Q333631"},{"title":"Sweater","blocks":[{"text":"A sweater or pullover, also called a jersey or jumper, is a piece of clothing, typically with long sleeves, made of knitted or crocheted material that covers the upper part of the body. When sleeveless, the garment is often called a slipover, tank top, or sweater vest."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Selburose-sweater.jpg/330px-Selburose-sweater.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweater","wikidata":"Q232191"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Pashmina_goats.jpg/500px-Pashmina_goats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Pashmina_goats.jpg/500px-Pashmina_goats.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pashmina goats, Ladakh, India","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APashmina_goats.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Cashmeregoat_shearing.png/250px-Cashmeregoat_shearing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/83/Cashmeregoat_shearing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Cashmeregoat_shearing.png/250px-Cashmeregoat_shearing.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cashmere shearing. Corindhap, Australia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACashmeregoat_shearing.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Shawl_makers_in_Kashmir_%281867%29.jpg/500px-Shawl_makers_in_Kashmir_%281867%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Shawl_makers_in_Kashmir_%281867%29.jpg/500px-Shawl_makers_in_Kashmir_%281867%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An 1867 William Simpson painting depicting men manufacturing shawls using pashm wool","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A